  <section id="topology" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Topology tables</h2>
    <p>A <code>Brep</code> is a set of index tables. Entities refer to each other by index, never by object reference.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Entity</th><th>Holds</th><th>STEP counterpart</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Vertex</td><td>point, tolerance</td><td>VERTEX_POINT</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Edge</td><td>exact curve, [t0, t1] with t0 &lt; t1, v0 = C(t0), v1 = C(t1), tolerance</td><td>EDGE_CURVE</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Coedge</td><td>one use of an edge by a loop: edge index, reversed, optional p-curve in the face (u, v)</td><td>ORIENTED_EDGE + PCURVE</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Loop</td><td>closed cycle of coedges; material on the left seen from outside (outer CCW, holes CW)</td><td>EDGE_LOOP</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Face</td><td>region of one surface; reversed means the outward normal is −(S_u × S_v)</td><td>ADVANCED_FACE</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shell</td><td>connected set of faces; a closed shell bounds a volume</td><td>CLOSED_SHELL</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Seams (an edge used twice by the same face, such as the generator line of a full cylinder) and poles (a loop through a degenerate point, such as a cone apex) need no special entities. That is why the exact sphere has 2 vertices, 1 edge and 1 face, and the torus 1 vertex, 2 edges and 1 face.</p>
