  <section id="boolean" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Exact Booleans</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Intersect.</strong> Edges of each solid against the faces of the other (exact points by Newton), edges lying on the other solid&apos;s surfaces, and the intersection curves of every pair of non-coincident faces, refined onto both surfaces and recognised as lines and circles, otherwise fitted as cubic B-splines within the fit tolerance.</li>
      <li><strong>Imprint.</strong> Every intersection point becomes a vertex in one registry for both solids, edges are split there, and each face is cut by the curves on it through a planar arrangement in its (u, v) domain.</li>
      <li><strong>Classify.</strong> A point inside each fragment is located against the other solid: in, out, or on with the same or opposite orientation.</li>
      <li><strong>Sew.</strong> Fragments are kept by the operation&apos;s rules, coincident pieces become one edge, coplanar neighbours merge, and the result is validated. A result that is not a closed manifold solid throws <code>BooleanError</code>; it is never returned.</li>
    </ol>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="boolean.ts">
{"export const booleanBrep: (A: Brep, B: Brep, op: BooleanOp, options?: BooleanOptions) => BooleanResult\nexport interface BooleanOptions {\n  tol?: number          // coincidence, default 1e-8 \u00d7 model size, at least 1e-7 mm\n  fitTol?: number       // fitted intersection curves, default 1e-6 \u00d7 size, at least 1e-6 mm\n  seedAngleDeg?: number // normal turn per seed-mesh triangle, default 10\n  debug?: (stage: string, data: unknown) => void\n}\nexport interface BooleanResult {\n  brep: Brep; warnings: string[]\n  stats: { intersectionCurves: number; splitPoints: number; fragments: number; keptFaces: number }\n}"}
    </DocCode>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="Box minus cylinder — real output">
{"import { brep } from \u0022@cadcamfun/core\u0022\nconst { makeBox, makeCylinder, frame, booleanBrep, validateBrep, massProperties } = brep\n\nconst box = makeBox(60, 40, 15)\nconst hole = makeCylinder(6, 25, frame({ x: 30, y: 20, z: -5 }))\nconst r = booleanBrep(box, hole, \u0022subtract\u0022)          // 141 ms\nr.stats     // {\u0022intersectionCurves\u0022:2,\u0022splitPoints\u0022:12,\u0022fragments\u0022:13,\u0022keptFaces\u0022:7}\nr.warnings  // []\nvalidateBrep(r.brep)\n// {\u0022valid\u0022:true,\u0022manifoldSolid\u0022:true,\u0022genus\u0022:1,\u0022volume\u0022:34303.53996706151,\u0022issues\u0022:[]}\nmassProperties(r.brep).volume   // 34303.53996706151  = 60\u00b740\u00b715 \u2212 \u03c0\u00b76\u00b2\u00b715 exactly"}
    </DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="mass" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Mass properties</h2>
    <p>Volume, area, centroid and the inertia tensor (unit density, about the centroid, row-major) come from the divergence theorem over the boundary faces. Planar faces are integrated exactly by Green&apos;s theorem along the exact edge curves; curved faces are integrated on a parameter-step tessellation of the exact surface.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Body</th><th>massProperties</th><th>Closed form</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>box − Ø12 hole, volume</td><td>34303.53996706151</td><td>34303.539967061515</td></tr>
        <tr><td>same, area</td><td>8139.292006587684</td><td>8139.292006587698</td></tr>
        <tr><td>same, centroid</td><td>(30.000, 20.000, 7.500)</td><td>(30, 20, 7.5)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>sphere r10, volume</td><td>4188.790204786379</td><td>4188.790204786391</td></tr>
        <tr><td>torus 20/5, volume</td><td>9869.60440109475</td><td>9869.604401089358</td></tr>
        <tr><td>filleted plate, volume</td><td>34097.52220392302</td><td>34097.522203923065</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The filleted-plate closed form is the subtracted plate minus four corner slivers of (r² − πr²/4)·h = (16 − 4π)·15 each.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="features" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Fillets, chamfers and hole recognition</h2>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="Signatures">
{"export const blendCandidates: (b: Brep, minAngleDegrees?: number /* 0.5 */) => BlendCandidate[]\nexport const selectBlendEdges: (edges: ReadonlyArray<BlendCandidate>, s?: BlendSelector) => BlendCandidate[]\n// BlendSelector: parallel?: \u0022x\u0022|\u0022y\u0022|\u0022z\u0022, within?: box, kind?: \u0022convex\u0022|\u0022concave\u0022|\u0022both\u0022 (default convex), minLength?, curved?\nexport const blendBrep: (b: Brep, edges: ReadonlyArray<BlendCandidate>, size: number,\n  style?: \u0022fillet\u0022 | \u0022chamfer\u0022, options?: BlendOptions) => BlendResult\n// BlendResult: brep, blended, vertices (rolling-ball corners), rejected (edge ids), ...\nexport const recognizeHoles: (b: Brep, options?: HoleRecognitionOptions) => ReadonlyArray<RecognizedHole>"}
    </DocCode>
    <p>On the box − cylinder body there were 14 blend candidates; <code>selectBlendEdges(c, &#123; parallel: "z" &#125;)</code> picked the 4 vertical outer edges, and <code>blendBrep(b, sel, 4)</code> filleted all 4 with none rejected in 94 ms. The hole read back from the faces alone:</p>
    <DocCode language="json" title="recognizeHoles — real output">
{"[{\u0022entry\u0022:{\u0022x\u0022:30,\u0022y\u0022:20,\u0022z\u0022:15},\u0022direction\u0022:{\u0022x\u0022:0,\u0022y\u0022:0,\u0022z\u0022:-1},\u0022depth\u0022:15,\u0022diameter\u0022:12,\n  \u0022form\u0022:\u0022simple\u0022,\u0022bottom\u0022:\u0022through\u0022,\n  \u0022segments\u0022:[{\u0022kind\u0022:\u0022cylinder\u0022,\u0022start\u0022:0,\u0022end\u0022:15,\u0022diameterStart\u0022:12,\u0022diameterEnd\u0022:12,\u0022faces\u0022:[6]}]}]"}
    </DocCode>
    <p>Forms are <code>simple</code>, <code>counterbore</code> and <code>countersink</code>; bottoms are <code>through</code>, <code>flat</code> and <code>drill-point</code>. Recognition reads faces, not history, so a STEP import, a Boolean result and a parametric part are recognised alike.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="tessellation" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Watertight tessellation</h2>
    <p>Every edge is sampled once on its exact curve and every face that uses it shares those samples, so the welded mesh has no cracks. Faces are triangulated by constrained Delaunay in their (u, v) domain.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>chordTol</td><td>1/2000 of the model diagonal, clamped to [0.001, 0.05] mm</td><td>max distance between mesh and exact surface</td></tr>
        <tr><td>angleTolDeg</td><td>15</td><td>max normal turn across a triangle (0 disables)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>maxEdge</td><td>0 (none)</td><td>edge-length target</td></tr>
        <tr><td>maxPointsPerFace</td><td>200 000</td><td>safety cap</td></tr>
        <tr><td>paramStep</td><td>—</td><td>integration mode used by massProperties</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>For the filleted plate <code>defaultChordTol</code> returned 0.0368 mm and <code>tessellateBrep</code> produced 652 triangles plus per-face meshes, edge polylines and warnings.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="step" class="doc-section">
    <h2>STEP exchange</h2>
    <code>writeStepBreps</code> writes an AP214 (AUTOMOTIVE_DESIGN) file in millimetres and radians, one ADVANCED_BREP_SHAPE_REPRESENTATION per part. <code>readStepBreps</code> reads AP203, AP214 and AP242 geometry and topology, including assemblies with their transformations and product names. Unknown entities are reported in <code>warnings</code> and their faces skipped; nothing is guessed.
    <DocCode language="text" title="writeStepBreps([{ name: &quot;plate&quot;, brep }]) — first lines of the real 358-line file">
{"ISO-10303-21;\nHEADER;\nFILE_DESCRIPTION(('CADCAMFUN exact B-rep'),'2;1');\nFILE_NAME('plate','2026-10-15T04:49:21',(''),(''),'CADCAMFUN B-rep kernel','CADCAMFUN','');\nFILE_SCHEMA(('AUTOMOTIVE_DESIGN { 1 0 10303 214 1 1 1 1 }'));\nENDSEC;\nDATA;\n#1=APPLICATION_CONTEXT('core data for automotive mechanical design processes');\n#2=APPLICATION_PROTOCOL_DEFINITION('international standard','automotive_design',2000,#1);\n#3=PRODUCT_CONTEXT('',#1,'mechanical');\n#4=PRODUCT_DEFINITION_CONTEXT('part definition',#1,'design');\n#5=(LENGTH_UNIT()NAMED_UNIT(*)SI_UNIT(.MILLI.,.METRE.));"}
    </DocCode>
    <p>Reading that file back gave one part whose volume was 34097.52220392303 mm³, equal to the written body to 11 significant digits.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="budget" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Time budget</h2>
    <p>The kernel is synchronous and runs on the caller&apos;s thread. <code>withBudget(ms, f)</code> sets a deadline; long loops (face pairs of a Boolean, face splitting, surface intersection, tessellation) call <code>checkBudget</code>, which throws <code>BudgetExceeded</code>. Nested budgets keep the earlier deadline; without a budget a check costs one comparison.</p>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="Real output">
{"withBudget(1, () => booleanBrep(makeSphere(10), makeBox(10, 10, 10), \u0022union\u0022))\n// BudgetExceeded: the exact model exceeded its time budget of 1 ms"}
    </DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="troubleshooting" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>BooleanError</code></td><td>the sewn result is not a closed manifold solid (tangent or near-coincident faces)</td><td>move an operand slightly off tangency; check both with validateBrep first</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>BudgetExceeded</code></td><td>the operation took longer than its budget</td><td>simplify, or give the caller a larger budget</td></tr>
        <tr><td>RangeError from a primitive</td><td>non-positive size, or torus minor ≥ major</td><td>e.g. "torus minor radius must be smaller than the major radius"</td></tr>
        <tr><td>blend edge in <code>rejected</code></td><td>face too narrow for the radius, knife edge or slanted wall</td><td>smaller radius, or a chamfer</td></tr>
        <tr><td>STEP part missing faces</td><td>unsupported entity in the file</td><td>read <code>warnings</code> from readStepBreps</td></tr>
        <tr><td>validateBrep reports issues</td><td>index, orientation or edge-on-surface error in a built body</td><td>the issue names the entity; nothing is repaired automatically</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

